{! layout("base") !}
{! block("title") !}toke — A Language Built for AI{! end !}
{! block("content") !}

<section class="hero">
  <h1>A Language Built for <span>AI</span></h1>
  <p>A programming language purpose-built for LLM code generation. 40&ndash;75% fewer tokens. Faster inference. Lower cost.</p>
  <div class="cta">
    <a href="/docs" class="btn btn-primary">Get started</a>
    <a href="/docs/learn/01-why-toke" class="btn btn-outline">Why toke?</a>
    <a href="https://github.com/karwalski/toke" class="btn btn-outline">GitHub</a>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================================================================== -->
<!-- The Problem                                                        -->
<!-- ================================================================== -->

<div class="section-heading">
  <h2>The Problem</h2>
</div>
<p class="section-desc">Every time an AI writes code, it generates one token at a time. Each token costs compute, time, and money. Today's programming languages were designed for humans &mdash; they're full of verbose keywords, boilerplate imports, and ceremony that LLMs must produce token by token.</p>
<p class="section-desc"><strong style="color:#ccc">toke</strong> is a programming language designed from first principles to minimise the number of tokens an LLM needs to generate. With a purpose-built tokenizer, toke uses 40&ndash;75% fewer tokens than Python, C, or Java for equivalent complete programs &mdash; and the gap widens as documentation standards increase, because toke's LLM training corpus is comment-stripped.</p>

<div class="explainer" style="max-width:860px;margin:1rem auto;padding-left:2rem;padding-right:2rem;">
  <p><strong>What is a token?</strong> LLMs don't read characters &mdash; they read <em>tokens</em>, which are chunks of text (roughly 3&ndash;4 characters each). The statement <code>return 0;</code> costs 4 tokens every time an LLM writes it. In toke, the same statement is <code>&lt;0;</code> &mdash; and with a purpose-built tokenizer trained on toke programs, the entire return-and-close pattern <code>&lt;0;};</code> becomes a single token.</p>
</div>

<!-- ================================================================== -->
<!-- See the Difference                                                 -->
<!-- ================================================================== -->

<div class="section-heading">
  <h2>See the Difference</h2>
</div>

<div class="prompt-box">
  <span class="prompt-label">Prompt</span>
  <p>"Write a complete program that prints the 10th Fibonacci number"</p>
</div>

<p class="section-desc">toke is measured with its purpose-built BPE tokenizer. Other languages use cl100k_base (GPT-4) &mdash; the tokenizer their models actually use. Every example is a complete, runnable program.</p>

<div class="tv-tabs" style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto;padding:0 2rem;">
  <div class="tv-tab-bar">
    <button class="tv-tab-btn active" data-tab="see-toke">toke (24 tokens)</button>
    <button class="tv-tab-btn" data-tab="see-python">Python (41)</button>
    <button class="tv-tab-btn" data-tab="see-c">C (52)</button>
    <button class="tv-tab-btn" data-tab="see-java">Java (55)</button>
  </div>

  <div class="tv-tab-panel active" id="see-toke">
    <div class="token-viz toke-p2">
      <pre class="tv-code"><code><span class="tv-tok tv-c0">m=fib</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c1">;i=io:std.io</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c2">;f=</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c3">fib</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c4">(n:i64):i64{if(n</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c5">&lt;2){&lt;</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c6">n;</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c7">}el{&lt;</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c8">fib</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c9">(n</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c10">-1)+</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c11">fib</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c0">(n</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c1">-2</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c2">);</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c3">}};</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c4">f=main():</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c5">i64{</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c6">io.println</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c7">(</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c8">fib(</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c9">10</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c10">));&lt;</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c11">0;};</span></code></pre>
      <div class="tv-footer">
        <span class="token-badge toke-p2">24 tokens &mdash; purpose-built toke BPE (16K vocab, measured)</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="tv-note" style="margin:0.75rem 0;font-size:0.85rem;color:#888;line-height:1.4;">
      This is the complete program &mdash; module declaration, import, function, main, and I/O. Token boundaries are measured using toke's purpose-built BPE tokenizer (16,384 tokens, trained on 25,953 toke programs). Patterns like <code style="background:#161616;border:1px solid #222;border-radius:3px;padding:0.1em 0.3em;font-size:0.82em;color:#c792ea;">;i=io:std.io</code> (entire import as 1 token) and <code style="background:#161616;border:1px solid #222;border-radius:3px;padding:0.1em 0.3em;font-size:0.82em;color:#c792ea;">(n:i64):i64{if(n</code> (signature + conditional in 1 token) emerge naturally from BPE training on real toke code. <a href="/tokenizer" style="color:#c792ea;">Try the live tokenizer &rarr;</a>
    </div>
  </div>

