<div class="comparison-note">
  <p><strong>Why different tokenizers?</strong> toke uses its purpose-built BPE (24 tokens, measured) because that is how a toke-native model tokenizes programs. Python, C, and Java are measured with cl100k_base because that is the tokenizer used by the models generating them. Each language measured with the tokenizer its model actually uses. The toke BPE (16,384 tokens) was trained on 25,953 toke programs and averages 52% fewer tokens than cl100k across 42 benchmarks. <a href="/tokenizer" style="color:#c792ea;">Try it yourself &rarr;</a></p>
</div>

<!-- ================================================================== -->
<!-- With Best Practices                                                -->
<!-- ================================================================== -->

<div class="section-heading">
  <h3>With Best Practices</h3>
</div>
<p class="section-desc">Real-world code isn't minimal. Teams add documentation comments, descriptive names, and type annotations. Here's the same fibonacci program written to production standards &mdash; and how that affects token cost.</p>

<div class="tv-tabs" style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto;padding:0 2rem;">
  <div class="tv-tab-bar">
    <button class="tv-tab-btn active" data-tab="bp-toke">toke (24 &mdash; unchanged)</button>
    <button class="tv-tab-btn" data-tab="bp-python">Python (82)</button>
    <button class="tv-tab-btn" data-tab="bp-c">C (75)</button>
    <button class="tv-tab-btn" data-tab="bp-java">Java (102)</button>
  </div>

  <div class="tv-tab-panel active" id="bp-toke">
    <div class="token-viz toke-p2">
      <pre class="tv-code"><code><span class="tv-tok tv-c0">m=</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c1">fib;</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c2">i=</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c3">io:</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c4">std.io;</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c5">f=</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c6">fib(</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c7">n:i64):i64{</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c8">if(</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c9">n&lt;2){&lt;</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c10">n;}</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c11">el{&lt;</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c0">fib(</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c1">n-1)+</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c2">fib(</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c3">n-2</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c4">);}}</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c5">;</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c6">f=</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c7">main():i64{</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c8">io.println(</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c9">fib(10</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c10">));</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c11">&lt;0;};</span></code></pre>
      <div class="tv-footer">
        <span class="token-badge toke-p2">24 tokens &mdash; identical to above (toke has no comment syntax)</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="tv-note" style="margin:0.75rem 0;font-size:0.85rem;color:#888;line-height:1.4;">
      The program is identical. toke supports comments via <code style="background:#161616;border:1px solid #222;border-radius:3px;padding:0.1em 0.3em;font-size:0.82em;color:#c792ea;">(* ... *)</code> for human authors, but the LLM training corpus is comment-stripped &mdash; so AI-generated code carries zero documentation overhead. There are no docstrings to inflate, no boilerplate that grows with coding standards. The type signatures <em>are</em> the documentation.
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="tv-tab-panel" id="bp-python">
    <div class="token-viz other">
      <pre class="tv-code"><code><span class="tv-tok tv-c0">def</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c1"> fibonacci</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c2">(number</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c3">:</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c4"> int</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c5">)</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c6"> -&gt;</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c7"> int</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c8">:&#x21B5;
</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c9">   </span><span class="tv-tok tv-c10"> &quot;&quot;&quot;</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c11">Return</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c0"> the</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c1"> n</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c2">-th</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c3"> Fibonacci</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c4"> number</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c5">.&quot;&quot;&quot;&#x21B5;
</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c6">   </span><span class="tv-tok tv-c7"> if</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c8"> number</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c9"> &lt;</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c10"> </span><span class="tv-tok tv-c11">2</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c0">:&#x21B5;
</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c1">       </span><span class="tv-tok tv-c2"> return</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c3"> number</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c4">&#x21B5;
</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c5">   </span><span class="tv-tok tv-c6"> return</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c7"> fibonacci</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c8">(number</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c9"> -</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c10"> </span><span class="tv-tok tv-c11">1</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c0">)</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c1"> +</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c2"> fibonacci</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c3">(number</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c4"> -</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c5"> </span><span class="tv-tok tv-c6">2</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c7">)&#x21B5;
&#x21B5;
&#x21B5;
</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c8">def</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c9"> main</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c10">()</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c11"> -&gt;</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c0"> None</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c1">:&#x21B5;
</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c2">   </span><span class="tv-tok tv-c3"> &quot;&quot;&quot;</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c4">Print</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c5"> the</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c6"> </span><span class="tv-tok tv-c7">10</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c8">th</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c9"> Fibonacci</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c10"> number</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c11">.&quot;&quot;&quot;&#x21B5;
</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c0">   </span><span class="tv-tok tv-c1"> result</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c2"> =</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c3"> fibonacci</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c4">(</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c5">10</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c6">)&#x21B5;
</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c7">   </span><span class="tv-tok tv-c8"> print</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c9">(result</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c10">)&#x21B5;
&#x21B5;
&#x21B5;
</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c11">if</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c0"> __</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c1">name</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c2">__</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c3"> ==</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c4"> &quot;__</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c5">main</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c6">__&quot;:&#x21B5;
</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c7">   </span><span class="tv-tok tv-c8"> main</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c9">()</span></code></pre>
      <div class="tv-footer">
        <span class="token-badge other">82 tokens &mdash; cl100k_base &mdash; with docstrings and main guard</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="tv-note" style="margin:0.75rem 0;font-size:0.85rem;color:#888;line-height:1.4;">
      Adding one-line docstrings and an <code style="background:#161616;border:1px solid #222;border-radius:3px;padding:0.1em 0.3em;font-size:0.82em;color:#c792ea;">if __name__</code> guard doubles the token count: 41 &rarr; 82 tokens. With full Args/Returns/Raises docstrings, this grows further.
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="tv-tab-panel" id="bp-c">
    <div class="token-viz other">
      <pre class="tv-code"><code><span class="tv-tok tv-c0">#include</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c1"> &lt;</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c2">stdio</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c3">.h</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c4">&gt;&#x21B5;
&#x21B5;
</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c5">/**&#x21B5;
</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c6"> *</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c7"> Return</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c8"> the</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c9"> n</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c10">-th</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c11"> Fibonacci</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c0"> number</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c1">.&#x21B5;
</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c2"> */&#x21B5;
</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c3">long</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c4"> fibonacci</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c5">(long</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c6"> number</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c7">)</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c8"> {&#x21B5;
</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c9">   </span><span class="tv-tok tv-c10"> if</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c11"> (</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c0">number</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c1"> &lt;</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c2"> </span><span class="tv-tok tv-c3">2</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c4">)</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c5"> return</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c6"> number</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c7">;&#x21B5;
